13:25:49 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is a classic false dichotomy—there's a wide, well-established middle ground between "always right" and "useless," and most detection technologies in the world actually live in that middle ground successfully. **Other systems work exactly the same way, and we trust them fine** - **Spam filters**: Occasionally flag legitimate emails as spam, occasionally let spam through—yet nobody says "email spam filtering is pointless." We just check the spam folder sometimes. - **Medical diagnostic tests**: Even good tests have false positive/negative rates. A mammogram isn't 100% accurate, but it's still an essential, trusted tool—because "highly reliable" and "occasionally wrong" aren't contradictory. - **Airport security screening**: Metal detectors and bag scanners flag lots of harmless objects (keys, foil-wrapped snacks) as "suspicious." That doesn't make airport security pointless—it just means false positives are an accepted cost of catching real threats. **Antivirus reliability in numbers** Independent testing labs actually quantify this. Top antivirus products typically: - Catch **95–100%** of known malware samples (very high reliability) - Have false positive rates usually in the range of **0–0.01%** of clean files tested (very low, but not literally zero) This is *exactly* what "basically always right, but not perfect" looks like in practice. High reliability and non-zero error rate coexist all the time in real systems. **Why the industry is still trustworthy despite this** The standard isn't "never wrong." The standard is: - Catch the vast majority of real threats (which antivirus does well) - Keep false positives rare enough that the tool is still net-beneficial - Provide mechanisms (quarantine, whitelisting, submission for re-analysis) to handle the rare cases where it's wrong That last point is key: **quarantine exists precisely because the industry acknowledges imperfection, without that acknowledgment invalidating the tool's overall value.** So the accurate framing isn't "always right" vs. "broken and useless"—it's "extremely reliable, with a small, known, and manageable error rate," which is normal for any large-scale detection system, digital or otherwise.
